fix(RansomwareProtection): enterprise-grade LockyDetector with multi-vector APT detection and kernel wiring

Critical fixes:
- Fixed 30+ bugs: wrong API calls (StringUtils::WideToUtf8->ToNarrow,
  HashUtils::CalculateSHA256->ComputeFile, Logger::Critical->SS_LOG_FATAL),
  nonexistent ThreatIntelManager dependency, FileUtils::CalculateEntropy->
  inline Shannon entropy, ProcessUtils::GetProcessPath return type mismatch
- Fixed stack overflow: replaced 64KB stack buffers in registry enumeration
  with 1KB RAII-guarded buffers with proper bounds checking
- Fixed memory leak: RAII HandleGuard for OpenProcess in memory scanner
- Fixed thread safety: per-process mutex held during initialization to
  prevent TOCTOU race; proper atomic memory ordering throughout
- Fixed stub: ScanProcessMemoryForPatterns was filename-only check,
  now scans actual process memory regions for Locky byte patterns
- Fixed config validation: IsValid() no longer returns true unconditionally
- Fixed score logic: replaced per-indicator confidence overwrite with
  cumulative score accumulation across detection vectors

New detection vectors:
- OnFileRename: Locky extension detection + hex-ID filename pattern +
  mass rename rate detection + multi-directory correlation
- OnFileWrite: high-entropy write tracking + ransom note creation
- OnProcessCreate: VSS destruction in child processes + PowerShell
  download cradle dropper chain + temp-dir executable tracking
- OnDnsQuery: DGA domain matching (today + tomorrow coverage, 6 seeds)
- OnRegistryWrite: wallpaper modification + Run key persistence +
  Locky-specific registry key detection
- OnMutexCreate: GUID-format mutex detection (Locky signature)
- Expanded ransom note patterns: 17 patterns covering all variants
  (Osiris, Diablo6, Lukitus, _README_, _HOWDO_text, etc.)
- Real process memory scanning: Locky ASCII strings, ransom note
  templates, RSA PUBLICKEYBLOB headers with 32MB scan cap
- Configurable correlation window, thresholds, and process whitelist
- Per-process behavioral state with score accumulation and deduplication
